Dear Professor Ripley:

I now understand that mclust cannot be used with dissimilarity matricies. I
have constructed my distance matrix using SAS. This distance matrix is the
sum of nine euclidean distance matricies generated in SAS (one distance
matrix for each genetic locus of allele frequency information).

My dataset has nine genetic loci of information across thirty-seven human
populations. Good clustering results have been generated with the SAS
package with proc cluster and in Splus with agnes.

At this point I am trying to decide the common problem of how many clusters
and which clustering algorithm is best for this dataset. Work by Fraley and
Raftery: http://www.stat.washington.edu/fraley/mclust/reps.shtml outlines
how model based cluster analysis can be implemented in Splus to test several
distributional assumptions about the covariance matrix and look at the
geometric interpretations of those matricies. I would like to use this
approach to evaluate Ward's method, as well as Sokal and Michener's
average-linkage method of clustering by means of the Bk measure outlined in
a paper by E.B. Fowlkes and C.L. Mallows "A Method for comparing two
hierarchical clusterings", Journal of the American Statistical Association,
September 1983, Volume 78, Number 383, pp553-568.

The  problem that is slowing my progress is the ability to test different
distributional assumptions with this data as i have outlined above. I may
need to create an Splus dataset with my original raw data which is nxp,
however due to the dataset complexity and my rather naive abilities with
Splus it will take some time I am afraid.

The example given in the help section was entered as it apprears. I still
get the error. Is it possible that some defaults in my package may be
causing this problem. I have also downloaded updated mclust algorithms from
Fraley and Raftery but have not been able to use them due to errors.

Thank-You for considering this problem

Greggory S. LaBerge
Univ. Colorado Health Sciences Centre
Denver, CO, USA

