From gibianskyl at globomax.com  Tue Oct  3 12:27:15 2000
From: gibianskyl at globomax.com (Gibiansky, Leonid)
Date: Tue, 03 Oct 2000 08:27:15 -0400
Subject: RE: SPLUS from DOS system command-line
Message-ID: <3B4EC6D7963DD4118B2E00A0C96F6A4D0FE396@globomax.com>

One of the explanations may be the difference in interpretation of if blocks
from the command line and from the script. Consider two examples:

Example 1
 if(3 > 2) {print("3 > 2") } else {print("3 < 2")}
        
Example 2                       
if(3 > 2) 
       {
        print("3 > 2")
       } 
     else {
            print("3 < 2")
           }
>From script, they both print "3 > 2" (which is true)

>From the command line (and I think from the DOS as well) you have

if(3 > 2) {print("3 > 2") } else {print("3 < 2")}
[1] "3 > 2"
>                               
> if(3 > 2) 
+        {
+         print("3 > 2")
+        } 
[1] "3 > 2"
>      else {
Error in parse(text = txt): Syntax error: "else" used illegally at this
point:
else
>           print("3 < 2")
[1] "3 < 2"
>          }
Error in parse(text = txt): Syntax error: No opening parenthesis, before "}"
at this point:

The system cannot analyze the script and cannot see that it is continued on
the next line.  S+ evaluates TRUE and FALSE branches of the if else block.

The remedy is to write in on one line, as in the first example, or include
the whole expression in {} as here:

{
if(3 > 2) 
       {
        print("3 > 2")
       } 
     else {
            print("3 < 2")
           }
}

Leonid

