Grid3D adds planes to 3D graphs (Splus 2000 Prof R 2, Prof Ripley).  
Potentially it's a wonderful visualization aid,
nearly indispensible since there are no depth cues for the eye to interpret.
Unfortunately, it appears that the display algorithm is to display the plane
first,
then display the graph (surface) without regard to which is closer to the
"eye".  Ideally,
the portion of the grid that is between the graph and the eye in XYZ-space
would be seen,
and would hide or change the shading of the portion of the surface that is
supposed
to be behind.  Without that, the 3D plane seems nearly pointless. The
programming challenge of 
doing it right does not appear any worse than, say, redrawing the surface
when it's rotated,
which appears to be well done.  Determining the intersection of a plane with
a
surface must already be done now to create the grid surface displays.
I'm sure there are hidden complications, for example involving 
multiple planes or multiple surfaces, but maybe not fatal complications.

I've spoken with a very nice gal at support at mathsoft support, who
suggested that a request
to provide this feature might be placed somewhere on the priority list for
the imminent new
release if there is enough interest.  So, if you are interested, you may
want to state 
your opinion now.

Meanwhile, are there any solutions, even partial, current in our community?

