From warnes at biostat.washington.edu  Wed Oct  4 19:57:32 2000
From: warnes at biostat.washington.edu (Gregory R. Warnes)
Date: Wed, 04 Oct 2000 19:57:32 +0000
Subject: Re: Where is the max value?
In-Reply-To: <Pine.GSO.4.10.10010041013580.8832-100000@ev.nssl.noaa.gov>
References: <Pine.GSO.4.10.10010041013580.8832-100000@ev.nssl.noaa.gov>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.21.0010041936210.1918-100000@hydra.warnes.net>

I don't think that there is a built in function that does what you
want.  I think this function will do what you want:

where.max _ function(x)
{
   max.val _ max(x)

   row.mat _ matrix( 1:(dim(x)[1]), nrow=dim(x)[1], ncol=dim(x)[2], byrow=F)
   col.mat _ matrix( 1:(dim(x)[2]), nrow=dim(x)[1], ncol=dim(x)[2], byrow=T)
   return( cbind(row=row.mat[max.val==x],col=col.mat[max.val==x]) )
}

For example:

> y _ matrix(c(1, 1, 1, 1, 
+            1, 1, 2, 3, 
+            1, 2, 3, 2, 
+            1, 3, 2, 1),
+            nrow = 4, ncol = 4, byrow=TB)
> y
     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] 
[1,]    1    1    1    1
[2,]    1    1    2    3
[3,]    1    2    3    2
[4,]    1    3    2    2
> where.max(y)
     row col 
[1,]   4   2
[2,]   3   3
[3,]   2   4
> y[where.max(y)]
[1] 3 3 3

-Greg

