On Thu, 5 Oct 2000 aczarn at cs.uwa.edu.au wrote:

> to anyone who can assist
> 
> i am having a curious problem with aov.
> 
> when i run a model such as 
> 
> > galgOA21<-aov(generationFirstAt1.0E.6~(crValueO*muValueO + 
> seedPopulationF + seedCrossoverF + seed
> MutationF), data = galgO)
> 
> the resultant output is devoid of some of the factors
(I think you mean terms.)

Take note of the message:

> 18 out of 407 effects not estimable

That means that there are collinearities in your data, and I suspect that
you have what is known as extrinsic aliasing, and that usually indicates
an inappropriate analysis for the experimental design.

The interaction crValueO*muValueO is using a lot of degrees of freedom,
and a likely story is that the `missing' factors are constant in
the cells of crValueO*muValueO.  Try alias() to find out what is going on,
and talk to whoever designed the experiment about an appropriate analysis.
You can't recover information not in the experiment!

It's one of those toss-up issues. lm() refuses to handle rank-deficient
models unless singular.ok=T (and that puzzles people), whereas aov()
assumes that aliasing was intentional and merely notes its extent and
removes aliased terms. I think its designers must have assumed that users
would be using the correct model for their design.  (They certainly did for
multi-stratum analyses.)

[... examples ...]

