Thanks for the responses from Brian Ripley, Frank Harrell, Judi Scheffer,
and Andy Cooper.

It looks like the best way to fit a multinomial logistic regression with
multiply imputed data is via Professor Ripley's multinom function in
conjunction with Professor Harrell's fit.mult.impute function.  There were
also suggestions pointing to Joe Schaffer's multiple imputation routine's at
www.stat.psu.edu/~jls/.  Analyses on the separate data sets can then be
combined using Rubin's rules (outlined in his book "Multiple Imputation for
Non Response in Surveys" (1987) and also in Schaffer's book and several
articles).  Lastly, there was a recommendation for LIMDEP as being a good
program for fitting multinomial logit models.

