From m1jjh00 at frb.gov  Fri Oct  6 19:46:01 2000
From: m1jjh00 at frb.gov (Jeffrey J. Hallman)
Date: Fri, 06 Oct 2000 15:46:01 -0400
Subject: List indexing in 5.1
Message-ID: <200010061946.PAA04768@mrpmx1.rsma.frb.gov>

One feature I used quite a bit in 3.4 has disappeared in 5.1.  I refer to list
indexing, described in the 3.4 help page as follows:

  A special case of the [[ construct is when x is  recursive
  (e.g.,  a  list)  and  i  is  a list.  The components of i
  should all have length 1 and be character or numeric.  The
  statement x[[i]] is equivalent to:

      x [[ i[[1]] ]] [[ i[[2]] ]]  ...  [[ i[[length(i)]] ]]

  For example, x[[list("b", 2, 4)]] will pick  out  the  4th
  component of the 2nd component of the b component of x.

I have found this very useful in getting at deeply nested structures, but in
Splus5.1, trying to do this results in an error message to the effect that the
list (the one acting as an index) could not be coerced to integer.

So I whipped up a couple of functions (listIndex() and listIndexAssignment(), 
see below) and that mimic the old behavior.  But now I have another problem.
When I look at methods already set for "[[", this is what I get:

> showMethods("[[")
     Database                 x 
[1,] "splus"  "ANY"            
[2,] "splus"  "groupVecVirtual"
[3,] "splus"  "numericSequence"
[4,] "splus"  "timeRelative"   
[5,] "splus"  "timeSequence"   

So it would appear that I can't write a method with a signature that depends
of the classes of both x and the index argument "...".  Is there a way around
this?

Jeff

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

listIndex <- function(aRecursive, aListIndex){
  for(i in 1:length(aListIndex))
    aRecursive <- aRecursive[[aListIndex[[i]]]]
  return(aRecursive)
}

listIndexAssignment <- function(aRecursive, aListIndex, value){
  exprString <- substitute(aRecursive)
  for(index in aListIndex){
    if(is.numeric(index)){
      if(abs(index - round(index)) < 0.00001)
        index <- round(index)
      else
        stop(paste("Noninteger index:", index))
      exprString <- paste(exprString, "[[", index, "]]", sep = "")
    }
    else {
      if(is.character(index))
        exprString <- paste(exprString, "[[\"", index, "\"]]", sep = "")
      else
        stop("Index elements must be character or integer")
    }
  }
  expr <- parse(text = paste(exprString, "<-", deparse(substitute(value))))
  eval(parse(text=expr), local = sys.parent(1))
}

