No. Both are working correctly (that is, as documented). Don't expect large
numbers printed to limited accuracy to have all their printed digits
accurate.  1845 to three significant digits is 1840 (using round-to-even).
You would not want

110,373,561

printed as that would you?  1.10e8 or 110,000,000, I think indicate the
actual precision much better.

For reasons I have never fathomed, some S-PLUS print and summary methods
reduce the accuracy from that set in options(digits).  Just because
digits=7 (the default) is normally too much, that is no reason to penalize
users who set it to a sensible value (like 5 or even 4 as below).
But we are stuck with it.  An opportunity was lost to choose sensible
precisions automatically when a lot of these things were coded, and that's
one thing I have to teach every year: don't quote S-PLUS output verbatim
but think about sensible precision when you typeset it.

