(Whether this works depends on the class of model.object and hence the
predict method used.  You've omitted that crucial piece of information!)

> First, I tried using na.action=na.exclude and na.rm=T in the call to
> predict to no avail.  Then I tried creating a logical vector which would

Well, read the documentation for the predict method, as I doubt if they are
supported.

> use an F to indicate rows with NAs.  However, the logical vector put NAs
> instead of Fs for rows which had NAs.  Finally, I used
> !is.na(indep.data.DF), but it returned a logical data frame with 4
> columns of Ts and Fs.  I think I need a vector (i.e. 1 column) of
> logicals that uses a T to indicate rows with no missing values and an F
> to indicate rows that have one or more NAs.  I can then use the call
> 
> >predict(model.object, indep.data.DF[logical.vector])
> 
> to get some predictions.  At least that's what I think I need.  Thoughts
> and comments appreciated.

I posted a solution to that very problem yesterday!  Use na.omit to find
the set of rows containing NAs, and exclude them.  You can also do
something like predict.ppr in library(MASS), which has

                newdata <- as.data.frame(newdata)
                rn <- row.names(newdata)
                Terms <- delete.response(object$terms)
                m <- model.frame(Terms, newdata, na.action = na.omit)
                keep <- match(row.names(m), rn)

and m is a suitable dataframe for use as newdata in predict, as it
has dropped the columns which are actually used and contain NAs.
keep is the set of indices which appear in m, and is used to arrange for
the result to be of the expected size but have NAs in the rows where
prediction cannot be done.

One day maybe all the standard methods will handle NAs in prediction as
cleanly as predict.ppr.  S-PLUS 2000 has made a start with the napredict
function, but that only applies to predictions on the original dataset in
the method (predict.lm) I looked at.

