From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Tue, 10 Oct 2000 13:49:35 +0100
Subject: Re: Using .dll from Fortran
Message-ID: <200010101249.NAA25515@toucan.stats.ox.ac.uk>

> From: "Fox, G.J.A. (EDTE)" <G.J.A.Fox at edte.utwente.nl>
> Date: Tue, 10 Oct 2000 10:28:37 +0200
> 
> I have problem with loading a dll made in Digital Visual Fortran 6 using
> dll.load in S+ 2000.
> I can load the dll in S+ on the PC where Fortran 6 is also installed with
> Win 98.
> But I cannot load it in S+ 2000 on any other PC with Win 98 or Win 95, 
> then S+ gives the statement -1157. I have checked if S+ could find the dll
> with is.dir and files.in.dir. 

This usually means that the DLL is linked against other DLLs (the
Fortran run-time DLLs) that cannot be found.  Try using dumpbin /imports
on your DLL (I am assuming you also have VC++6.0 installed, and hence have 
dumpbin) to see what your DLL import and from what, then put the missing DLLs
into the same directory as your DLL.

I've often seen this with people who use the Cygwin compilers which rely
on cygwin1.dll, and indeed have made that mistake myself more than once.

There's a program pedump.exe that you can search for on the Web that
does the same job as dumpbin if you don't have VC++6.0.

