From William.Venables at cmis.CSIRO.AU  Wed Oct 11 06:26:41 2000
From: William.Venables at cmis.CSIRO.AU (Bill Venables)
Date: Wed, 11 Oct 2000 16:26:41 +1000
Subject: Re: contrasts for fully crossed anova
In-Reply-To: <39E348F8.FD25C672@rci.rutgers.edu>
References: <39E348F8.FD25C672@rci.rutgers.edu>
Message-ID: <200010110626.RAA21337@snowy.nsw.cmis.CSIRO.AU>

Martin H H Stevens asks:

> I am running windows 98 with SPLUS 2000 prof.
> I am trying to get the contrasts for an 4 x 3 x 2 anova for a planned
> experiment. 

> I can do it for a single factor:
> e.g.,
> > contrasts(CA98$Fert)
>    [,1] [,2] [,3]
>  0   -1   -1   -1
>  8    1   -1   -1
> 16    0    2   -1
> 32    0    0    3

This is the contrast matrix that will be used unless you supply
something else.

> 
> but then I try  two or more factors  to no avail:
> > contrasts(CA98$Fert * CA98$LH)

It's very hard to know what you mean, but I guess it goes something
like this:  "If I fit a two-factor model, what contrasts do the
coefficients I get represent?"  There is no direct tool to give you
that information (and the answer is quite complex) but you can piece
together the information yourself by looking at the model matrix:

        model.matrix(~ Fert * LH, CA98)

You might also care to study section 6.2 (p. 154ff) of MASS.
Parameter interpretation is directly addressed, but the discussion is
brief.  We tried very hard to lay this stuff out as clearly as we
could, but although it is not really difficult, the level of detail is
such that in the end there is no substitute for really studying the
material yourself and working through a few examples in detail.

> Error in Ops.factor(CA98$Fert, CA98$LH): "*" not meaningful for factors
> 
> Last I heard "*" was really meaningful for factors....

That depends on which "*" you are talking about, there are at least
two.  In linear model formulae the "*" operator is used for defining
factorial designs with interactions so it certainly is meaningful.
However in arithmetic expressions, as you have here, factors are
specifically disallowed because they define classifications and as
such are not meaningful in arithmetic.  So all the arithmetic
operators are not meaningful for factors but all the linear model
formula operators are.  Had you put a ~ in front you would have not
have got this particular error message (but you would certainly have
got another).

