Subject: Re: Fw: QUERY: The 0.632+ Bootstrap method
References: <002701c03367$b53a0360$9c97bd89@csu.cuhk.edu.hk>
Message-ID: <39E468E3.9AEBDC45@virginia.edu>

Ewout Steyerberg (steyerberg at mgz.fgg.eur.nl) has a special version
of the validate.lrm function for the Design library that does this.
He has also done a lot of simulations comparing the ordinary
bootstrap with 632 and 632+.  632+ seems to have nothing to offer
in terms of mean squared error.  Same for 632.  I think the bottom
line is that Efron and Tibshirani's research on this has been
for classification error only; advantages of the newer bootstraps
do not seem to carry over to other (better) indexes of predictive
ability.  Percent classified correctly has a host of fundamental
statistical problems associated with it.  -Frank Harrell

